# Wills Act 1838 — formal validity, revocation, revival and the privileged will

## Section 1 — Short title

<!-- l4: decide:s 1 — short title -->

**1.** S 1 — short title means This Act is the Wills Act 1838..

## Section 2 — Interpretation

<!-- l4: decide:s 2 — interpretation -->

**2.** S 2 — interpretation if—

    (a) “internal law”, in relation to any territory or state, means the law which would apply in a case where no question of the law in force in any other territory or state arose;;

    (b) “personal estate” shall extend to leasehold estates and other chattels real, and also to moneys, shares of Government and other funds, securities for money, not being real estates, debts, choses in action, rights, credits, goods and all other property whatsoever which by law devolves upon the executor or administrator, and to any share or interest therein;;

    (c) “real estate” shall extend to messuages, lands, rents and hereditaments, whether corporeal, incorporeal or personal, and to any undivided share thereof and to any estate, right or interest, other than a chattel interest, therein;;

    (d) “state” means a territory or group of territories having its own law of nationality;; and

    (e) “will” includes a testament and an appointment by will or by writing in the nature of a will in exercise of a power and also a disposition by will and testament and any other testamentary disposition..

## The facts this Act asks that the shared ontology does not carry

<!-- l4: declare:Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act -->

**3.** In this Act, “Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where it was executed”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (b) “the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time when the will was executed”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (c) “the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time of his death”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (d) “the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator habitually resided at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (e) “the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the state of which the testator was a national at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (f) “the will was executed on board a vessel or an aircraft of any description, and the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory with which, having regard to its registration (if any) and other relevant circumstances, the vessel or aircraft may be taken to have been most closely connected”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (g) “so far as the will disposes of immovable property, its execution conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the property was situated”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (h) “so far as the will revokes a will which under this Act would be treated as properly executed, or revokes a provision which under this Act would be treated as comprised in a properly executed will, the execution of the later will conformed to any law by reference to which the revoked will or provision would be treated as properly executed”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (i) “so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:The position of the signature -->

**4.** In this Act, “The position of the signature” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “there is a signature of the testator, or of a person signing for him in his presence and by his direction”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (b) “the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), is so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (c) “the signature does not follow and is not immediately after the foot or end of the will”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (d) “a blank space intervenes between the concluding word of the will and the signature”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (e) “the signature is placed among the words of the testimonium clause or of the clause of attestation, or follows or is after or under the clause of attestation, either with or without a blank space intervening, or follows or is after, or under, or beside the names or one of the names of the subscribing witnesses”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (f) “the signature is on a side or page or other portion of the paper or papers containing the will whereon no clause or paragraph or disposing part of the will is written above the signature”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (g) “there appears to be sufficient space on or at the bottom of the preceding side or page or other portion of the same paper on which the will is written to contain the signature”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:A disposition, and where it stands in relation to the signature -->

**5.** In this Act, “A disposition, and where it stands in relation to the signature” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “the disposition”, being a Disposition;

    (b) “underneath or following the signature”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (c) “inserted after the signature was made”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:The ways in which a will may have been revoked -->

**6.** In this Act, “The ways in which a will may have been revoked” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (b) “revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (c) “burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by the testator”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (d) “burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by some person in his presence and by his direction”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (e) “destroyed with the intention of revoking it”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:An alteration made in a will after its execution -->

**7.** In this Act, “An alteration made in a will after its execution” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “the words or effect of the will before the alteration are apparent”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (b) “the alteration is executed in the like manner as by this Act is required for the execution of the will”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (c) “the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made in the margin or on some other part of the will opposite or near to the alteration”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (d) “the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made at the foot or end of or opposite to a memorandum referring to the alteration and written at the end or some other part of the will”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:The revival of a revoked will -->

**8.** In this Act, “The revival of a revoked will” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “revived by the re-execution thereof”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (b) “revived by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (c) “partly revoked, and afterwards wholly revoked”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (d) “an intention to the contrary is shown”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks -->

**9.** In this Act, “A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “the will”, being a Will;

    (b) “the position of the signature”, being a The position of the signature;

    (c) “the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act”, being a Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act;

    (d) “the ways in which it has been revoked”, being a The ways in which a will may have been revoked;

    (e) “its revival”, being a The revival of a revoked will;

    (f) “the signature was made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time, counting only witnesses to whom and to whose wife or husband no such disposition is given or made”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (g) “a conveyance or other act has been made or done subsequently to its execution, of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (h) “that conveyance or other act is an act by which the will is revoked as aforesaid”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (i) “a contrary intention appears by the will as to the time from which it speaks”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (j) “a contrary intention appears by the will as to the lapse of a devise or bequest to a child or other issue”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (k) “made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid”, being a BOOLEAN.

<!-- l4: declare:A case under this Act -->

**10.** In this Act, “A case under this Act” means a record consisting of—

    (a) “the deceased”, being a Deceased person;

    (b) “the will in question”, being an A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks;

    (c) “an application for an order under section 28(1) has been made”, being a BOOLEAN;

    (d) “the date of that application”, being an optional DATE;

    (e) “a court has given permission for the application to be made after the end of that period”, being a BOOLEAN; and

    (f) “a grant limited to the remainder of the testator’s estate has previously been made or is made at the same time”, being a BOOLEAN.

## Helpers — dates, survivorship and descent

<!-- l4: decide:the will under consideration -->

**11.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the will under consideration means the case's the will in question's the will.

<!-- l4: decide:the testator of the will under consideration -->

**12.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the testator of the will under consideration means the will under consideration with the case's the testator.

<!-- l4: decide:the date of the death of -->

**13.** In relation to a Person “the person”, the date of the death of means consider the case distinctions of the person's vital status :  when living has  then NOTHING. when died on has the date of death then JUST with the date of death. when dead, the date of death not known has  then NOTHING.

<!-- l4: decide:falls strictly after -->

**14.** In relation to an optional DATE “the date” and a DATE “the day fixed by the Act”, falls strictly after means consider the case distinctions of the date :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the day then Day with the day is greater than Day with the day fixed by the Act.

<!-- l4: decide:falls on or after -->

**15.** In relation to an optional DATE “the date” and a DATE “the day fixed by the Act”, falls on or after means consider the case distinctions of the date :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the day then Day with the day is at least Day with the day fixed by the Act.

<!-- l4: decide:is proved to fall strictly before -->

**16.** In relation to an optional DATE “the earlier” and an optional DATE “the later”, is proved to fall strictly before means consider the case distinctions of the earlier :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the first day then consider the case distinctions of the later :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the second day then Day with the first day is less than Day with the second day.

<!-- l4: decide:the twenty-first anniversary of -->

**17.** In relation to a DATE “the date of birth”, the twenty-first anniversary of means Date with DATE_DAY with the date of birth, DATE_MONTH with the date of birth and the sum of DATE_YEAR with the date of birth and 21.

<!-- l4: decide:six months after -->

**18.** In relation to a DATE “the date”, six months after means add months with the date and 6.

<!-- l4: decide:the earliest of -->

**19.** In relation to a list of DATE “the dates”, the earliest of means consider the case distinctions of the dates :  when EMPTY has  then NOTHING. when the first is followed by the rest then consider the case distinctions of the earliest of with the rest :  when NOTHING has  then JUST with the first. when JUST has the other then if Day with the first is at most Day with the other then JUST with the first else JUST with the other.

<!-- l4: decide:was living on -->

**20.** In relation to a Person “the person” and a DATE “the day”, was living on means consider the case distinctions of the person's vital status :  when living has  then TRUE. when died on has the date of death then Day with the date of death is greater than Day with the day. when dead, the date of death not known has  then FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:the descendants bearing the reference -->

**21.** In relation to a Person “the ancestor” and a STRING “the reference”, the descendants bearing the reference means concat with map with given the relation then if the relation's the child's the reference for this person is equal to the reference then list of the relation's the child else the descendants bearing the reference with the relation's the child and the reference and the ancestor's children.

<!-- l4: decide:some descendant was living on -->

**22.** In relation to a Person “the ancestor” and a DATE “the day”, some descendant was living on means any with given the relation then was living on with the relation's the child and the day or some descendant was living on with the relation's the child and the day and the ancestor's children.

<!-- l4: decide:the reference for the donee -->

**23.** In relation to a Person or corporation “the donee”, the reference for the donee means consider the case distinctions of the donee :  when an individual has the individual then the individual's the reference for this person. in any other case.

## Section 4 — Will of infant invalid

<!-- l4: declare:Whether the testator had attained 21 years of age when the will was made -->

**24.** In this Act, “Whether the testator had attained 21 years of age when the will was made” means one of the following—

    (a) “under 21 years of age”;

    (b) “21 years of age or over”; or

    (c) “not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known”.

<!-- l4: decide:the age of the testator when the will was made -->

**25.** In relation to a Will “the will”, the age of the testator when the will was made means consider the case distinctions of the will's the testator's date of birth :  when NOTHING has  then not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known. when JUST has the date of birth then consider the case distinctions of the will's the date of execution :  when NOTHING has  then not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known. when JUST has the date of execution then if Day with the date of execution is less than Day with the twenty-first anniversary of with the date of birth then under 21 years of age else 21 years of age or over.

<!-- l4: decide:s 4 — will of infant invalid -->

**26.** In relation to a Will “the will”, s 4 — will of infant invalid if—

    (a) No will made by any person under 21 years of age shall be valid.; and

    (b) the age of the testator when the will was made with the will is equal to under 21 years of age.

<!-- l4: decide:s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over -->

**27.** In relation to a Will “the will”, s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over means the age of the testator when the will was made with the will is equal to 21 years of age or over.

## Section 5 — Rules as to formal validity

<!-- l4: decide:s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed -->

**28.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed if—

    (a) A will shall be treated as properly executed if its execution conformed to the internal law in force —;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (a); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where it was executed;

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b)(i); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time when the will was executed;

    (d) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b)(ii); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time of his death;

    (e) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator habitually resided at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b); or

    (f) all of the following apply—

        (i) (d); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the state of which the testator was a national at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b).

For the purposes of this provision—

“the connecting factors” means the case's the will in question's the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act;

<!-- l4: decide:s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed -->

**29.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed if—

    (a) Without prejudice to subsection (2), the following shall be treated as properly executed:;

    (b) (a); and

    (c) the case's the will in question's the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act's the will was executed on board a vessel or an aircraft of any description, and the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory with which, having regard to its registration (if any) and other relevant circumstances, the vessel or aircraft may be taken to have been most closely connected.

<!-- l4: decide:s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it -->

**30.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it if—

    (a) Without prejudice to subsection (2), the following shall be treated as properly executed:;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's so far as the will disposes of immovable property, its execution conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the property was situated;

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's so far as the will revokes a will which under this Act would be treated as properly executed, or revokes a provision which under this Act would be treated as comprised in a properly executed will, the execution of the later will conformed to any law by reference to which the revoked will or provision would be treated as properly executed; or

    (d) all of the following apply—

        (i) (d); and

        (ii) the connecting factors's so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the connecting factors” means the case's the will in question's the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act;

<!-- l4: decide:s 5(4) — the will is treated as improperly executed by reason only that its execution was not in accordance with formal requirements contained in the instrument creating the power -->

**31.** S 5(4) — the will is treated as improperly executed by reason only that its execution was not in accordance with formal requirements contained in the instrument creating the power if—

    (a) A will so far as it exercises a power of appointment shall not be treated as improperly executed by reason only that its execution was not in accordance with any formal requirements contained in the instrument creating the power.; and

    (b) FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator -->

**32.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator if—

    (a) This section shall not apply to a will of a testator who died before 26 June 1992 and shall apply to a will of a testator who dies after that date whether the will was executed before or after that date.; and

    (b) falls strictly after with the date of the death of with the testator of the will under consideration with the case and YMD with 1992, 6 and 26.

<!-- l4: decide:s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9) -->

**33.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9) if—

    (a) s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed with the case;

    (b) s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed with the case; or

    (c) s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it with the case.

<!-- l4: declare:The extent to which the will is treated as properly executed under section 5 -->

**34.** In this Act, “The extent to which the will is treated as properly executed under section 5” means one of the following—

    (a) “not treated as properly executed under section 5”;

    (b) “treated as properly executed”; or

    (c) “treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment”.

<!-- l4: decide:s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed -->

**35.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed means if not s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator with the case then not treated as properly executed under section 5 if s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed with the case or s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed with the case then treated as properly executed if s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it with the case then treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment otherwise not treated as properly executed under section 5.

<!-- l4: decide:s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed -->

**36.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed if—

    (a) This section shall take effect notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act.; and

    (b) s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed with the case is equal to treated as properly executed.

<!-- l4: decide:s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment -->

**37.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment if—

    (a) This section shall take effect notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act.;

    (b) Without prejudice to subsection (2), the following shall be treated as properly executed:;

    (c) (d) a will so far as it exercises a power of appointment, if the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power.;

    (d) s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator with the case; and

    (e) any of the following applies—

        (i) s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed with the case;

        (ii) s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed with the case; or

        (iii) the case's the will in question's the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act's so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power.

## Section 6 — Mode of execution

<!-- l4: decide:at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator -->

**38.** In relation to a Will “the will”, at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator means count with filter with given the attesting witness then the attesting witness's subscribed the will in the presence of the testator and the will's the attesting witnesses is at least 2.

<!-- l4: decide:s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature -->

**39.** In relation to an A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks “the will and its further facts”, s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature if—

    (a) Every will shall, as far only as regards the position of the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), be deemed to be valid under this section if the signature shall be so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will, that it shall be apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will;;

    (b) the position's there is a signature of the testator, or of a person signing for him in his presence and by his direction;

    (c) the position's the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), is so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will; and

    (d) the will and its further facts's the will's apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the position” means the will and its further facts's the position of the signature;

<!-- l4: decide:s 6(2) — the manner of execution -->

**40.** In relation to an A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks “the will and its further facts”, s 6(2) — the manner of execution if—

    (a) Every will shall be signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator, or by some other person in his presence and by his direction,;

    (b) any of the following applies—

        (i) the instrument's signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator;

        (ii) the instrument's signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction; or

        (iii) s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature with the will and its further facts;

    (c) and the signature shall be made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time,;

    (d) the instrument's the signature made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time;

    (e) and those witnesses shall subscribe the will in the presence of the testator,;

    (f) at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator with the instrument;

    (g) but no form of attestation shall be necessary.; and

    (h) TRUE.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the instrument” means the will and its further facts's the will;

<!-- l4: decide:s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2) -->

**41.** In relation to an A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks “the will and its further facts”, s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2) if—

    (a) No will shall be valid unless it is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2).;

    (b) the will and its further facts's the will's in writing; and

    (c) s 6(2) — the manner of execution with the will and its further facts.

<!-- l4: decide:one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present -->

**42.** In relation to a The position of the signature “the position”, one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present if—

    (a) all of the following apply—

        (i) (a); and

        (ii) the position's the signature does not follow and is not immediately after the foot or end of the will;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b); and

        (ii) the position's a blank space intervenes between the concluding word of the will and the signature;

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c); and

        (ii) the position's the signature is placed among the words of the testimonium clause or of the clause of attestation, or follows or is after or under the clause of attestation, either with or without a blank space intervening, or follows or is after, or under, or beside the names or one of the names of the subscribing witnesses;

    (d) all of the following apply—

        (i) (d); and

        (ii) the position's the signature is on a side or page or other portion of the paper or papers containing the will whereon no clause or paragraph or disposing part of the will is written above the signature; or

    (e) all of the following apply—

        (i) (e); and

        (ii) the position's there appears to be sufficient space on or at the bottom of the preceding side or page or other portion of the same paper on which the will is written to contain the signature.

<!-- l4: decide:s 6(3)(a)-(e) — the will is affected by any of the enumerated circumstances -->

**43.** In relation to a The position of the signature “the position”, s 6(3)(a)-(e) — the will is affected by any of the enumerated circumstances if—

    (a) and no such will shall be affected by the circumstance —;

    (b) one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present with the position;

    (c) The enumeration of the circumstances under subsection (3) shall not restrict the generality of that subsection;; and

    (d) FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:s 6(4) — the signature is operative to give effect to the disposition -->

**44.** In relation to an A disposition, and where it stands in relation to the signature “the disposition and its position”, s 6(4) — the signature is operative to give effect to the disposition if—

    (a) but no signature under this Act shall be operative to give effect to any disposition or direction which is underneath or which follows it, nor shall it give effect to any disposition or direction inserted after the signature shall be made.; and

    (b) none of the following applies—

        (i) the disposition and its position's underneath or following the signature; or

        (ii) the disposition and its position's inserted after the signature was made.

## The will is properly executed — s 5 or s 6

<!-- l4: decide:the will is properly executed -->

**45.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the will is properly executed if—

    (a) s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed with the case; or

    (b) s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2) with the case's the will in question.

## Section 7 — Execution of appointment by will

<!-- l4: decide:s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act -->

**46.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act if—

    (a) No appointment made by will, in exercise of any power, shall be valid, unless the will is executed in the manner required by this Act.;

    (b) the will under consideration with the case's made in exercise of a power of appointment;

    (c) s 6(1) — the will is not in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2) with the case's the will in question; and

    (d) s 5 — the will is not treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment with the case.

<!-- l4: decide:s 7(2) — the will is, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, a valid execution of a power of appointment by will -->

**47.** In relation to an A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks “the will and its further facts”, s 7(2) — the will is, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, a valid execution of a power of appointment by will if—

    (a) Every will executed in the manner required by this Act shall, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, be a valid execution of a power of appointment by will, notwithstanding that it shall have been expressly required that a will made in exercise of that power should be executed with some additional or other form of execution or solemnity.; and

    (b) s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2) with the will and its further facts.

## Section 8 — Publication of will not necessary

<!-- l4: decide:s 8 — the will is valid without any other publication thereof -->

**48.** In relation to an A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks “the will and its further facts”, s 8 — the will is valid without any other publication thereof if—

    (a) Every will executed in the manner required by this Act shall be valid without any other publication thereof.; and

    (b) s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2) with the will and its further facts.

## Sections 9, 10(2), 11 and 12 — the competency of an attesting witness

<!-- l4: decide:the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will -->

**49.** In relation to a Will “the will” and an Attesting witness “the witness”, the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will if it is not the case that the witness's incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof, otherwise than by reason of sections 10(2), 11 and 12.

<!-- l4: decide:a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof -->

**50.** In relation to a Will “the will”, a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof means any with given the attesting witness then not the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will with the will and the attesting witness and the will's the attesting witnesses.

<!-- l4: decide:s 9 — the will is invalid by reason that a person who attests its execution is incompetent to be admitted a witness -->

**51.** In relation to a Will “the will”, s 9 — the will is invalid by reason that a person who attests its execution is incompetent to be admitted a witness if—

    (a) If any person who attests the execution of a will shall, at the time of the execution thereof or at any time afterwards, be incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof, the will shall not on that account be invalid.;

    (b) a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof with the will; and

    (c) FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) -->

**52.** In relation to an Attesting witness “the witness”, a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) if—

    (a) the witness's given or made a beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment by the will, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt; or

    (b) the witness's the wife or husband of the witness given or made such a beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment.

<!-- l4: decide:s 10(2) — the gift is a ground on which the attesting witness is not to be admitted a witness -->

**53.** In relation to an Attesting witness “the witness”, s 10(2) — the gift is a ground on which the attesting witness is not to be admitted a witness if—

    (a) The attesting witness referred to in subsection (1) shall be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will or to prove the validity or invalidity thereof, notwithstanding the devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment mentioned in the will.;

    (b) a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) with the witness; and

    (c) FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:s 11 — the charge is a ground on which the creditor is not to be admitted a witness -->

**54.** In relation to an Attesting witness “the witness”, s 11 — the charge is a ground on which the creditor is not to be admitted a witness if—

    (a) In case by any will any real or personal estate shall be charged with any debt, and any creditor, or the wife or husband of any creditor, whose debt is so charged, shall attest the execution of the will, the creditor notwithstanding the charge shall be admitted a witness to prove the execution of the will or to prove the validity or invalidity thereof.;

    (b) the witness's a creditor, or the wife or husband of a creditor, whose debt is charged on the estate by the will; and

    (c) FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will -->

**55.** In relation to a Will “the will” and an Attesting witness “the witness”, the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will means any with given the executor then the executor's the executor's the reference for this person is equal to the witness's the witness's the reference for this person and the will's the executors appointed by the will.

<!-- l4: decide:s 12 — being an executor of the will is a ground on which the person is not to be admitted a witness -->

**56.** In relation to a Will “the will” and an Attesting witness “the witness”, s 12 — being an executor of the will is a ground on which the person is not to be admitted a witness if—

    (a) No person shall, on account of his being an executor of a will, be incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution of the will or to prove the validity or invalidity thereof.;

    (b) the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will with the will and the witness; and

    (c) FALSE.

## Section 10 — Gifts to attesting witness or to wife or husband of attesting witness to be void

<!-- l4: decide:s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator -->

**57.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator if—

    (a) Subsection (3) shall apply to the will of any person dying after 29 May 1992, whether executed before or after 29 May 1992.; and

    (b) falls strictly after with the date of the death of with the testator of the will under consideration with the case and YMD with 1992, 5 and 29.

<!-- l4: decide:the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person -->

**58.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person if—

    (a) the will under consideration with the case's in writing;

    (b) any of the following applies—

        (i) the will under consideration with the case's signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator;

        (ii) the will under consideration with the case's signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction; or

        (iii) s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature with the case's the will in question;

    (c) the case's the will in question's the signature was made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time, counting only witnesses to whom and to whose wife or husband no such disposition is given or made; and

    (d) count with filter with given the attesting witness then not a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) with the attesting witness and the attesting witness's subscribed the will in the presence of the testator and the will under consideration with the case's the attesting witnesses is at least 2.

<!-- l4: decide:s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1) -->

**59.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1) if—

    (a) The attestation of a will by a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) shall be disregarded for the purposes of that subsection if the will is duly executed without his attestation and without that of any other such person.;

    (b) s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator with the case; and

    (c) the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person with the case.

<!-- l4: decide:s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void -->

**60.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case” and an Attesting witness “the witness”, s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void if—

    (a) If any person attests the execution of any will to whom or to whose wife or husband any beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment of or affecting any real or personal estate, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt, shall be thereby given or made, the devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment shall, so far only as concerns the person attesting the execution of the will, or the wife or husband of that person, or any person claiming under that person or wife or husband, be utterly null and void.;

    (b) a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) with the witness; and

    (c) s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is not disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1) with the case.

<!-- l4: decide:s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void -->

**61.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case” and a Disposition “the disposition”, s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void if—

    (a) any beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment of or affecting any real or personal estate, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt;

    (b) the disposition's beneficial;

    (c) the disposition is not a charge or direction for the payment of a debt; and

    (d) any with given the attesting witness then the attesting witness's the witness's the reference for this person is equal to the reference for the donee with the disposition's the donee and s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void with the case and the attesting witness and the will under consideration with the case's the attesting witnesses.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the disposition is a charge or direction for the payment of a debt” means consider the case distinctions of the disposition's the kind of disposition :  when a charge or direction for the payment of a debt has  then TRUE. in any other case FALSE;

## Section 13 — Will to be revoked by marriage except in certain cases

<!-- l4: decide:s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment -->

**62.** In relation to a Will “the will”, s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment if—

    (a) except a will made in exercise of a power of appointment, when the real or personal estate thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967.;

    (b) the will's made in exercise of a power of appointment; and

    (c) the will's the estate thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967.

<!-- l4: decide:s 13(1) — the will is revoked by the marriage -->

**63.** In relation to a Will “the will” and a Marriage “the marriage”, s 13(1) — the will is revoked by the marriage if—

    (a) Every will made by a man or woman shall be revoked by his or her marriage,; and

    (b) it is not the case that s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment with the will.

<!-- l4: decide:the marriage was solemnised after the will was made -->

**64.** In relation to a Will “the will” and a Marriage “the marriage”, the marriage was solemnised after the will was made means is proved to fall strictly before with the will's the date of execution and the marriage's the date of the solemnisation of the marriage.

<!-- l4: decide:s 13(2) — the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage -->

**65.** In relation to a Will “the will” and a Marriage “the marriage”, s 13(2) — the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage if—

    (a) Notwithstanding subsection (1), where a will made on or after 29 August 1938 is expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage, the will shall not be revoked by the solemnisation of the marriage contemplated; and this subsection shall apply notwithstanding that the marriage contemplated may be the first, second or subsequent marriage of a person lawfully practising polygamy.;

    (b) falls on or after with the will's the date of execution and YMD with 1938, 8 and 29;

    (c) the will's expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage; and

    (d) the marriage contemplated is this marriage.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the marriage contemplated is this marriage” means consider the case distinctions of the will's the reference for the person with whom the marriage contemplated was to be solemnised :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the intended then the intended is equal to the marriage's the spouse's the reference for this person;

<!-- l4: decide:s 13 — the will is revoked by this marriage -->

**66.** In relation to a Will “the will” and a Marriage “the marriage”, s 13 — the will is revoked by this marriage if—

    (a) s 13(1) — the will is revoked by the marriage with the will and the marriage;

    (b) the marriage was solemnised after the will was made with the will and the marriage; and

    (c) s 13(2) — the will is not expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage with the will and the marriage.

<!-- l4: decide:s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage -->

**67.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage means any with given the marriage then s 13 — the will is revoked by this marriage with the will under consideration with the case and the marriage and the case's the deceased's the family's the marriages of the deceased.

## Section 14 — No will to be revoked by presumption from altered circumstances

<!-- l4: decide:s 14 — the will is revoked by a presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in circumstances -->

**68.** S 14 — the will is revoked by a presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in circumstances if—

    (a) No will shall be revoked by any presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in circumstances.; and

    (b) FALSE.

## Section 15 — Revocation of will or codicil

<!-- l4: decide:s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it -->

**69.** In relation to a The ways in which a will may have been revoked “the ways”, s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it if—

    (a) by the burning, tearing, or otherwise destroying the will by the testator, or by some person in his presence and by his direction, with the intention of revoking it.;

    (b) any of the following applies—

        (i) the ways's burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by the testator; or

        (ii) the ways's burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by some person in his presence and by his direction; and

    (c) the ways's destroyed with the intention of revoking it.

<!-- l4: decide:s 15 — the will has been revoked -->

**70.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 15 — the will has been revoked if—

    (a) No will or codicil, or any part thereof, shall be revoked otherwise than —;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (a); and

        (ii) s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage with the case;

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b); and

        (ii) the ways's revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required;

    (d) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c); and

        (ii) the ways's revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed; or

    (e) all of the following apply—

        (i) (d); and

        (ii) s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it with the ways.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the ways” means the case's the will in question's the ways in which it has been revoked;

## Section 16 — Effect of obliteration, interlineation or alteration

<!-- l4: decide:s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed -->

**71.** In relation to an An alteration made in a will after its execution “the alteration”, s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed if—

    (a) A will referred to in subsection (1), with such alteration as part thereof, shall be deemed to be duly executed if the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses be made in the margin or on some other part of the will opposite or near to the alteration or at the foot or end of or opposite to a memorandum referring to the alteration and written at the end or some other part of the will.;

    (b) the alteration's the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made in the margin or on some other part of the will opposite or near to the alteration; or

    (c) the alteration's the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made at the foot or end of or opposite to a memorandum referring to the alteration and written at the end or some other part of the will.

<!-- l4: decide:s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect -->

**72.** In relation to an An alteration made in a will after its execution “the alteration”, s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect if—

    (a) No obliteration, interlineation or other alteration made in any will after the execution thereof shall be valid or have any effect except so far as the words or effect of the will before such alteration shall not be apparent, unless the alteration shall be executed in the like manner as by this Act is required for the execution of the will.;

    (b) the alteration's the words or effect of the will before the alteration are not apparent;

    (c) the alteration's the alteration is executed in the like manner as by this Act is required for the execution of the will; or

    (d) s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed with the alteration.

## Section 17 — Revival of revoked will

<!-- l4: declare:The extent of the revival -->

**73.** In this Act, “The extent of the revival” means one of the following—

    (a) “not revived”;

    (b) “revived, save as to so much thereof as was revoked before the revocation of the whole thereof”; or

    (c) “revived in whole”.

<!-- l4: decide:s 17(1) — the will has been revived -->

**74.** In relation to a The revival of a revoked will “the revival”, s 17(1) — the will has been revived if—

    (a) No will or codicil, or any part thereof, which shall be in any manner revoked, shall be revived otherwise than by the re-execution thereof, or by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil.;

    (b) the revival's revived by the re-execution thereof; or

    (c) the revival's revived by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil.

<!-- l4: decide:s 17 — the extent of the revival -->

**75.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 17 — the extent of the revival means if not s 15 — the will has been revoked with the case then not revived if not s 17(1) — the will has been revived with the revival then not revived if the revival's partly revoked, and afterwards wholly revoked and not the revival's an intention to the contrary is shown then revived, save as to so much thereof as was revoked before the revocation of the whole thereof otherwise revived in whole.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the revival” means the case's the will in question's its revival;

## Section 18 — Subsequent conveyance or other acts not to prevent operation of will

<!-- l4: decide:s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation -->

**76.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation if—

    (a) No conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of a will of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised, except an act by which the will shall be revoked as aforesaid, shall prevent the operation of the will with respect to the estate or interest in the real or personal estate as the testator shall have power to dispose of by will at the time of his death.;

    (b) the case's the will in question's a conveyance or other act has been made or done subsequently to its execution, of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised;

    (c) the case's the will in question's that conveyance or other act is an act by which the will is revoked as aforesaid; and

    (d) any of the following applies—

        (i) the ways's revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required;

        (ii) the ways's revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed; or

        (iii) s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it with the ways.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the ways” means the case's the will in question's the ways in which it has been revoked;

## Section 19 — Will to be construed to speak from death of testator

<!-- l4: declare:The time from which the will speaks -->

**77.** In this Act, “The time from which the will speaks” means one of the following—

    (a) “as if it had been executed immediately before the death of the testator”; or

    (b) “otherwise, a contrary intention appearing by the will”.

<!-- l4: decide:s 19 — the time from which the will is construed to speak -->

**78.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 19 — the time from which the will is construed to speak means if the case's the will in question's a contrary intention appears by the will as to the time from which it speaks, then otherwise, a contrary intention appearing by the will; otherwise, as if it had been executed immediately before the death of the testator.

## Section 26 — Gifts to children or other issue who leave issue living at testator’s death not to lapse

<!-- l4: decide:the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator -->

**79.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case” and a Disposition “the disposition”, the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator means the descendants bearing the reference with the testator of the will under consideration with the case and the reference for the donee with the disposition's the donee.

<!-- l4: decide:s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse -->

**80.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case” and a Disposition “the disposition”, s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse if—

    (a) Where any person being a child or other issue of the testator to whom any real or personal estate shall be devised or bequeathed for any estate or interest not determinable at or before the death of that person shall die in the lifetime of the testator leaving issue, and any such issue of that person shall be living at the time of the death of the testator, that devise or bequest shall not lapse, but shall take effect as if the death of that person had happened immediately after the death of the testator, unless a contrary intention shall appear by the will.;

    (b) it is not the case that null with the donee as issue;

    (c) the disposition's not determinable at or before the death of the donee;

    (d) the same person died in the lifetime of the testator, left issue, and some such issue was living at the testator’s death; and

    (e) it is not the case that the case's the will in question's a contrary intention appears by the will as to the lapse of a devise or bequest to a child or other issue.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the donee as issue” means the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator with the case and the disposition;

“the same person died in the lifetime of the testator, left issue, and some such issue was living at the testator’s death” means consider the case distinctions of the date of the death of with the testator of the will under consideration with the case :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the day of the death of the testator then any with given the person then is proved to fall strictly before with the date of the death of with the person and JUST with the day of the death of the testator and not null with the person's children and some descendant was living on with the person and the day of the death of the testator and the donee as issue;

## Section 27 — Saving as to wills of soldiers and mariners

<!-- l4: decide:s 27 — this section applies to the person -->

**81.** In relation to a Person “the person”, s 27 — this section applies to the person if—

    (a) Notwithstanding anything in this Act, any soldier being in actual military service, or any mariner or seaman being at sea,;

    (b) the person's a soldier being in actual military service;

    (c) the person's a mariner or seaman being at sea;

    (d) This section shall extend to any member of any naval or marine forces not only when he is at sea but also when he is so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service within the meaning of this section.; or

    (e) the person's a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service.

<!-- l4: decide:s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act -->

**82.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act if—

    (a) Notwithstanding anything in this Act, any soldier being in actual military service, or any mariner or seaman being at sea, may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act and may do so even though under 21 years of age.;

    (b) s 27 — this section applies to the person with the testator of the will under consideration with the case; and

    (c) the case's the will in question's made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid.

<!-- l4: decide:s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid -->

**83.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid if—

    (a) A testamentary disposition of any real estate made by a person to whom this section applies, and who dies after 29 August 1938 shall, notwithstanding that the person making the disposition was at the time of making it under 21 years of age or that the disposition has not been made in such manner or form as was on 29 August 1938 required by law, be valid in any case where the person making the disposition was of such age and the disposition has been made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid.;

    (b) s 27 — this section applies to the person with the testator of the will under consideration with the case;

    (c) falls strictly after with the date of the death of with the testator of the will under consideration with the case and YMD with 1938, 8 and 29; and

    (d) the case's the will in question's made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid.

<!-- l4: decide:the appointment is of a guardian of a child of the testator -->

**84.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case” and a Guardian “the appointment”, the appointment is of a guardian of a child of the testator means any with given the relation then the relation's the child's the reference for this person is equal to the appointment's the reference for the infant and the testator of the will under consideration with the case's children.

<!-- l4: decide:s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of full force and effect -->

**85.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of full force and effect means if falls strictly after with the date of the death of with the testator of the will under consideration with the case and YMD with 1938, 8 and 29 and s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act with the case or s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid with the case, then filter with given the appointment then the appointment is of a guardian of a child of the testator with the case and the appointment and the will under consideration with the case's the guardians of infant children appointed by the will; otherwise, EMPTY.

<!-- l4: decide:s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect -->

**86.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect if—

    (a) Where any person dies after 29 August 1938 having made a will which is, or which, if it had been a disposition of property, would have been rendered valid by this section, any appointment contained in that will of any person as guardian of the infant children of the testator shall be of full force and effect.; and

    (b) null with s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are not of full force and effect with the case.

<!-- l4: decide:the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good -->

**87.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good means map with given the appointment then the appointment's the guardian's the reference for this person and s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of full force and effect with the case.

<!-- l4: decide:the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate -->

**88.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate means map with given the appointment then the appointment's the guardian's the reference for this person and concat with map with given the infant then the infant's the guardians of the infant and the case's the deceased's the infants concerned in the estate.

## Section 28 — Rectification of will: the time within which an application must be made

<!-- l4: decide:the grant is limited to settled land or to trust property -->

**89.** In relation to a Grant “the grant”, the grant is limited to settled land or to trust property means consider the case distinctions of the grant's the limitation :  when limited to settled land or to trust property has  then TRUE. in any other case FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:the grant does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate -->

**90.** In relation to a Grant “the grant”, the grant does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate means consider the case distinctions of the grant's the limitation :  when a grant that does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate has  then TRUE. in any other case FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:the grant is limited to a part only of the testator’s estate -->

**91.** In relation to a Grant “the grant”, the grant is limited to a part only of the testator’s estate means consider the case distinctions of the grant's the limitation :  when limited to a part only of the testator’s estate has the part then TRUE. in any other case FALSE.

<!-- l4: decide:s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded -->

**92.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case” and a Grant “the grant”, s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded if—

    (a) The following grants are to be disregarded when considering, for the purposes of this section, when a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made:;

    (b) all of the following apply—

        (i) (a); and

        (ii) the grant is limited to settled land or to trust property with the grant;

    (c) all of the following apply—

        (i) (b); and

        (ii) the grant does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate with the grant; or

    (d) all of the following apply—

        (i) (c);

        (ii) the grant is limited to a part only of the testator’s estate with the grant; and

        (iii) the case's a grant limited to the remainder of the testator’s estate has previously been made or is not made at the same time.

<!-- l4: decide:the date on which the grant is made -->

**93.** In relation to a Grant “the grant”, the date on which the grant is made means if the grant's sealed under section 47(1) of the Probate and Administration Act 1934 and the grant is a probate or letters of administration with the will annexed, then the grant's the date of the sealing; otherwise, the grant's the date of the grant.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the grant is a probate or letters of administration with the will annexed” means consider the case distinctions of the grant's the kind of grant :  when probate has  then TRUE. when letters of administration with the will annexed has  then TRUE. in any other case FALSE;

<!-- l4: decide:the grants that are not to be disregarded -->

**94.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the grants that are not to be disregarded means filter with given the grant then not s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded with the case and the grant and the case's the deceased's the grants made in respect of the estate.

<!-- l4: decide:s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made -->

**95.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made means the earliest of with mapMaybe with the date on which the grant is made and the grants that are not to be disregarded with the case.

<!-- l4: declare:Whether an application for rectification is within the time allowed by section 28(2) -->

**96.** In this Act, “Whether an application for rectification is within the time allowed by section 28(2)” means one of the following—

    (a) “no application for an order under subsection (1) has been made”;

    (b) “within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made”;

    (c) “within the time allowed”;

    (d) “made with the permission of a court”;

    (e) “the date of the first such grant, or the date of the application, not being in evidence”; or

    (f) “made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made”.

<!-- l4: decide:s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time -->

**97.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time means if not the case's an application for an order under section 28(1) has been made then no application for an order under subsection (1) has been made if null with the grants that are not to be disregarded with the case then within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made if every grant that is not to be disregarded is dated and the application was made no later than 6 months after that date then within the time allowed if the case's a court has given permission for the application to be made after the end of that period then made with the permission of a court if the dates are not all in evidence then the date of the first such grant, or the date of the application, not being in evidence otherwise made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the first grant” means s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made with the case;

“every grant that is not to be disregarded is dated” means all with given the grant then isJust with the date on which the grant is made with the grant and the grants that are not to be disregarded with the case;

“the dates are not all in evidence” means not isJust with the first grant or not every grant that is not to be disregarded is dated or not isJust with the case's the date of that application;

“the application was made no later than 6 months after that date” means consider the case distinctions of the first grant :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the day of the grant then consider the case distinctions of the case's the date of that application :  when NOTHING has  then FALSE. when JUST has the day of the application then Day with the day of the application is at most Day with six months after with the day of the grant;

## Formal validity, all together

<!-- l4: declare:The formal validity of the will -->

**98.** In this Act, “The formal validity of the will” means one of the following—

    (a) “valid”;

    (b) “valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))”;

    (c) “not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)”;

    (d) “not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5”;

    (e) “treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of the will (section 5(3)(b), (c) or (d)), and not otherwise valid”;

    (f) “the age of the testator when the will was made not being in evidence, section 4 cannot be applied”; or

    (g) “the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied”.

<!-- l4: decide:the formal validity of the will -->

**99.** In relation to an A case under this Act “the case”, the formal validity of the will means if s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act with the case then valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1)) if s 4 — will of infant invalid with the will under consideration with the case then not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4) if not s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over with the will under consideration with the case then the age of the testator when the will was made not being in evidence, section 4 cannot be applied if the will is properly executed with the case then valid if the date of the death of the testator is not in evidence and s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9) with the case then the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied if s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed with the case is equal to treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment then treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of the will (section 5(3)(b), (c) or (d)), and not otherwise valid otherwise not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5.

For the purposes of this provision—

“the date of the death of the testator is not in evidence” means not isJust with the date of the death of with the testator of the will under consideration with the case;

